# Launches update.sh as an independent transient systemd unit rather than as
# a direct child process.
#
# Why this indirection exists: the one-click "System Upgrade" button in
# Admin -> Operations runs as a subprocess of eas-station-web.service. If
# update.sh ran as a direct descendant of that process, it would sit in
# eas-station-web.service's own cgroup -- and update.sh's own "Restarting
# Services" step (systemctl restart eas-station.target) would kill it
# mid-run along with the web worker that launched it, well before the
# script reaches its final summary. systemd-run gives update.sh its own
# unit, in its own cgroup, unaffected by that restart: it keeps running
# right through it, and its output keeps landing in the journal the whole
# time under a fixed, well-known unit name the web UI can poll with
# `journalctl -u eas-station-update` and `systemctl show
# eas-station-update.service` across the restart, from whichever worker
# process happens to answer the request next.
#
# --collect: the transient unit is garbage-collected once it exits (success
# or failure) instead of lingering as a "failed" unit that would block the
# next run from reusing the same name. That collection happens within
# seconds of exit -- fast enough that `systemctl show` on this unit cannot
# be trusted to still find it by the time anyone polls, success or failure.
# The web UI's progress endpoint deliberately treats the journal (which
# does not get cleaned up) as authoritative and only uses the live unit
# state as a "still running" signal.
#
# --no-block: return as soon as the unit is queued rather than waiting
# (potentially the whole multi-minute upgrade) for it to finish -- a
# Type=oneshot unit's start job is not considered done until the script
# exits, and this wrapper's caller does not want to sit on that.
#
# --property=Environment=HOME=/root: unlike `sudo`, which sets $HOME from
# the target user's passwd entry, a plain systemd-run transient unit does
# not set $HOME for the process it launches AT ALL -- confirmed directly on
# hardware (`env` inside an otherwise-identical transient unit shows no HOME
# entry whatsoever). update.sh runs as root, and with $HOME unset, every
# plain `git` command it runs (not the `sudo -u eas-station git ...` ones,
# which already get a correct $HOME from sudo) can't locate root's own
# global gitconfig at all: git's own error for that case is not "no such
# file", it's "fatal: $HOME not set", immediately followed by the exact
# same "detected dubious ownership" fatal every git subcommand emits when
# it can't establish the exception is configured -- indistinguishable from
# the config genuinely missing the entry, which is what made this so slow
# to track down: `git branch --show-current`, called bare (no `sudo -u`,
# no `-c safe.directory=`) moments into the "Downloading Latest Version"
# step, was failing on a check that every isolated repro of the *next*
# command in the script kept passing.
set -euo pipefail

exec systemd-run \
    --unit=eas-station-update \
    --collect \
    --no-block \
    --property=Type=oneshot \
    --property=Environment=HOME=/root \
    -- /opt/eas-station/update.sh "$@"
